[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) is a type of security vulnerability that allows attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. These scripts can execute in the user's browser and can be used to steal sensitive information, hijack user sessions, or deface websites. XSS vulnerabilities typically occur when web applications improperly handle user input or data from external sources."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of XSS"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stored XSS (Persistent XSS): The malicious script is stored on the target server (e.g., in a database, message forum, or comment section). When a user visits the page, the script is executed."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reflected XSS (Non-Persistent XSS): The malicious script is injected into the HTTP request. The server reflects the script back to the user's browser, which then executes it. This often involves tricking a user into clicking a malicious link."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DOM-based XSS: The vulnerability exists in the client-side JavaScript code itself. The malicious script manipulates the DOM (Document Object Model) directly, without the server being involved in reflecting the payload."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"How XSS Works"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"An attacker identifies a web application that accepts user input without proper sanitization or encoding. The attacker crafts a malicious script, typically in JavaScript, and injects it into the input field. When the application processes and displays the input, the script is executed in the user's browser."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Example of a vulnerable JavaScript code snippet
const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const name = urlParams.get('name');
document.getElementById('greeting').innerHTML = 'Hello, ' + name + '!';

// If the URL is: example.com/?name=<script>alert(\'XSS\')</script>
// The alert will be executed."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Preventing XSS"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Input Validation: Validate all user input on the server-side. Reject or sanitize any input that does not conform to the expected format."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Output Encoding: Encode all user-supplied data before displaying it on a web page. This prevents the browser from interpreting the data as executable code. Use appropriate encoding functions for the context (e.g., HTML encoding, JavaScript encoding, URL encoding)."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Example of HTML encoding in JavaScript
function escapeHTML(str) {
  let div = document.createElement('div');
  div.appendChild(document.createTextNode(str));
  return div.innerHTML;
}

const userInput = '<script>alert(\'XSS\')</script>';
const safeOutput = escapeHTML(userInput);
document.getElementById('output').innerHTML = safeOutput;"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Content Security Policy (CSP): Implement CSP to control the resources that the browser is allowed to load. This can help prevent the execution of malicious scripts from untrusted sources."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Example of CSP header
// Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://example.com;"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use a Web Application Firewall (WAF): A WAF can help detect and block XSS attacks before they reach your application."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly Update Software: Keep your web application framework, libraries, and other software components up to date with the latest security patches."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"DOM-based XSS Prevention"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using `eval()`: Never use `eval()` or similar functions that execute arbitrary code from strings. These functions are a major source of DOM-based XSS vulnerabilities."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sanitize DOM Manipulation: When manipulating the DOM, use safe methods like `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` to avoid interpreting user input as HTML."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Vulnerable Code
document.getElementById('output').innerHTML = location.hash.substring(1);

// Safer Code
document.getElementById('output').textContent = location.hash.substring(1);"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"XSS is a serious security vulnerability that can have significant consequences. By understanding the different types of XSS attacks and implementing appropriate prevention measures, you can protect your web applications and users from harm. Always prioritize input validation, output encoding, and the principle of least privilege when developing web applications."}]
